   Why inline: the hover animation (see .amfc-illo-car in amfc-2026.css) animates individual
   parts of the drawing — coin, sparkles, car body. CSS in this document cannot reach inside an
   SVG loaded through <img>; that renders as an independent document with no access to our
   stylesheet, so any rule targeting its internals is silently inert. Inlining puts the nodes in
   the page's own DOM where they can be selected and animated.

   The group wrappers below (__shadow / __car / __wheel / __coin / __sparkle) are the only
   additions — every path, mask, clipPath and gradient is byte-identical to the original export
   in public/assets/images/car-loan.svg, which is retained as the source of truth. Regenerate
   with the same grouping if that file is ever re-exported from Figma.

   Class names are amfc- prefixed rather than the generic .car-group/.wheel/.shadow-ellipse the
   spec suggested: this stylesheet ships into AMFC's live app alongside their own custom.css, and
   bare names that generic are a collision risk (see CLAUDE.md).

   role="img" + aria-label replaces the <img alt> that no longer applies once inlined. */
